- `branch` (fork, kind, text, system, sees, optional, id, to, tools, sealed, produces, writes): a new step as another branch of a fork ('optional': true lets it fail without failing the item; 'sees' names its inputs: "task", step ids, or single fact or file names there before the fork; 'system' as insert's); its output goes into the nearest prompt after the join that can take it -- an agent's own prompt knob, or the one text knob an op fills facts into -- or where 'to' says (a prompt knob, or a step: an agent whose prompt is no knob gets a section for it); with neither, refused unless a step reads it; 'tools' as insert's (sealed); 'sealed' as insert's on a code step. E.g. {"op": "branch", "fork": "solve_fork", "kind": "agent", "text": "<what this branch does>"}
- `rewire` (step, sees, text): change what a step sees: "task" (the task's own input, once, as the model steps receive it: never a step's output or a knob's text (an op's output carries the knobs the op declares for it in `knob_outputs`, else every knob the op reads)), step ids (their outputs), or single fact or file names there before it (that input alone); 'text' gives an agent step new instructions too, in place: the same id, outputs and place in a fork or join (so a branch can work from the task alone); with 'text' and no 'sees' it keeps what it sees. E.g. {"op": "rewire", "step": "solve_choose", "sees": ["task"]}
- `loop` (step, back_to, judge, text, sees, tools, max_iterations, id, sealed): a judge after a step that can send it back with its reason -- or, with 'back_to', send the work back to an earlier agent step, so every step from there to the judge runs again (a fork in between whole); 'back_to' is one every run passes before the step, in the same per-item pass and fork branch: 'judge' agent (text = what to check; its answer starts with proceed or reloop, then why), code (text = Python returning {'verdict': 'proceed' or 'reloop', 'reason': why}) or a callable. The judge is handed what the step read and wrote, unless 'sees' says otherwise, as text (a binary or very long file only named); with 'tools' (an agent judge, as insert's) it runs sealed instead, with the files it sees in its workspace, and its reply starts with the verdict; with 'sealed' true on a code judge, it runs in the Task's tool environment sealed, as insert's 'sealed' on a code step. The reason reaches the step sent back, with that step's own last output; it is sent back at most 'max_iterations' times, so it runs at most that many times plus one (default 2: up to 3 runs; 1 to 5; afterwards the knob <gate>__max_iterations, the gate being <step>_check unless 'id' names it), then the run goes on. E.g. {"op": "loop", "step": "solve_choose", "judge": "agent", "text": "<what to check>", "max_iterations": 2}
- `route` (after, judge, text, to, sees, tools, id, sealed): a judge after a step chooses one labelled next step ('to': label -> step id or a new step, whose 'takes_over' rewrites an earlier step's outputs on that way); the branches rejoin; with no 'to', proceed or redo the step in place; 'tools' as loop's judge. E.g. {"op": "route", "after": "solve", "judge": "agent", "text": "<how to choose>", "to": {"proceed": "solve_join", "revise": {"kind": "agent", "text": "<what it does>"}}}
- `split` (step, into: one or two steps, each like {kind, text, sees}): replace a step by two new steps run in turn ('into' two), or by one ('into' one: replace); the last takes over its outputs. E.g. {"op": "split", "step": "solve_choose", "into": [{"kind": "agent", "text": "<first part>"}, {"kind": "agent", "text": "<second part>"}]}
- `merge` (steps, kind, text, system, id, tools, sealed): two consecutive steps as one, with the first's inputs and the second's outputs; 'system' as insert's; 'tools' as insert's (sealed), else a sealed step's own come along; 'sealed' as insert's on a code step. E.g. {"op": "merge", "steps": ["solve_choose", "solve_choose_next"], "text": "<what the merged step does>"}
- `fallback` (step, kind, text, system, sees, id, call, reads, tools, sealed): a step run instead when 'step' (one run per item) fails -- it raises, crashes past its retries, hits its output limit, its turn cap, its prompt limit, or is still writing at the session time limit (backstop); never for a timeout (the harness's, a declared wall or the step's own) or a harness fault: it writes that step's outputs, so what reads them still gets an answer; 'kind' as in insert; it sees what 'step' sees; when the step writes more than one file, the fallback must be a code step or an agent with tools (a plain agent writes only its reply); 'sealed' as insert's on a code step. E.g. {"op": "fallback", "step": "solve_choose", "kind": "agent", "text": "<a simpler way to the same output>"}
- `map` (after, over, text, system, sees, max, combine, id, to, tools): after a step, a new agent step run once per element of 'over' (a list there: a fact, a file or a step's output), one model call each, all at once: a JSON array gives its items, a bulleted, numbered or lettered list its items, other text its non-empty lines; at most 'max' run (default 4, up to 8; past it the first 'max', and the combine is told so); 'text' says what to do with one element; 'combine' (a step as in insert) sees every result and writes one output, placed as insert's is; 'system' as insert's, for each run; 'tools' as insert's: each run sealed; remove the elements step or the combine and the whole map goes. E.g. {"op": "map", "after": "solve", "over": "solve", "text": "<what to do with one element>", "combine": {"kind": "agent", "text": "<how to combine the results>"}}

